/*
# Slider

A bounded number by drag. Same value contract as every control — `value` is the
attribute, **`input` owns the destination** (default: itself) — and the delivery
is continuous during a drag. Pressing the track jumps to that position (local
coordinates); the drag then follows the pointer by root-space delta
(`app.pointerX`), so it survives the pointer leaving the control. Arrow keys
nudge by `step`.

```declare
col: View [ x = 20, y = 20,
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 10 ],
    vol: Slider [ value = 40 ],
    Text [ text = { `volume ${Math.round(vol.value)}` } ]
    ]
```

## value
Where the handle sits, in your own units. Standalone the slider owns it; constrained
(`value = { app.volume }`) it needs an `input` override with it, or the drag's own
delivery is refused against the constraint.

## min
The value at the left end.

## max
The value at the right end.

## step
The quantum every delivered value snaps to, and the distance one arrow key moves.
`step = 0.01` for a fine continuous control; **it is never zero** — the quantizer
divides by it.

## input()
**Where the value goes**, and it fires **continuously through a drag**, not once at
release: the owner decides what to do with the stream, and the data layer owns any
coalescing. Override it to redirect the value (`input(v: number) { app.volume = v }`);
the default writes `value`.

## quant()
Snaps a raw value to `step` and clamps it into `min`…`max`. Every delivery routes
through it, so an override changes what the slider can produce — logarithmic scales,
detents, a magnetic centre.

## frac
The handle's position as 0…1, derived from the value and the range. What the track,
fill, and thumb all position from; read it to hang your own decoration off the handle.

## dragging
True from pointer-down until the captured release. **The `pressed` intrinsic
deliberately will not do here** — it is hit-chain-based, so it goes false the moment the
pointer leaves the thumb, while a drag must survive exactly that.

## _x0
**Internal.** The root-space pointer anchor captured at drag start.

## _v0
**Internal.** The value at drag start, that the pointer delta is applied to.

## focusShape()
Rings the circular grabber — the part the arrow keys move — rather than the whole track.

## barStyle()
True when the theme asks for the Material-3 rendition: a vertical bar handle with the
track shortened either side of it rather than drawn through. One theme token
(`provided("theme").sliderHandleStyle`) selects a rendition — **data, not a fork**.

## dotStyle()
True for the Fluent rendition: a round grabber with an accent dot inside that grows
under hover and press. The other value of the same token.

## onPointerDown
Jumps the value to the pressed position, claims focus, and latches the drag.
An override calls `super.onPointerDown(e)`.

## onPointerMove
Tracks the drag by **root-space** pointer delta, so the handle keeps up with a pointer
that has left the control.
An override calls `super.onPointerMove()`.

## onPointerUp
Ends the drag.
An override calls `super.onPointerUp()`: it is what ends the drag.

## onKeyDown
Home and End go to `min` and `max`; Page Up and Page Down move a tenth of the range, never
less than a `step`. The arrows step, as `Control` gives every slider.

## accessibilityIncrement()
One `step` up, through `quant` and `input` like any other change — what the Right and Up
arrows run (Control's keys), and what a screen reader's increment calls.

## accessibilityDecrement()
One `step` down — what Left and Down run.

## opacity
Disabled dims the whole control — every part alike, never a recolour — to the theme's `disabledOpacity`, or 0.45 when the theme states none: the one place the number lives.

## accessibilityValue
Follows `value`, in the slider's own units.

## accessibilityMin
Follows `min`.

## accessibilityMax
Follows `max`.

## accessibilityStep
Follows `step` — what one increment moves. A slider shows no name, so its use site sets `accessibilityLabel`.

## accessibilityLabel
The caption of a `Field` around it, unless the use site names the slider: its name to a screen
reader.

## fieldValue
Follows `value`: what `required` and `validate` judge (see `ValueControl`).
*/

include [ "valuecontrol.declare" ]

class Slider extends ValueControl [

    // what a screen reader is told: a slider, its value and range in the
    // slider's own units, named by whatever the use site names it

    accessibilityRole = slider,
    accessibilityLabel = { provided("fieldLabel", "") },
    accessibilityValue = { value },
    accessibilityMin = { min },
    accessibilityMax = { max },
    accessibilityStep = { step },

    // keyboard focus rings the circular grabber — the part the keys move

    focusShape() { return ({ x: this.thumb.x, y: this.thumb.y, w: this.thumb.width, h: this.thumb.height, rad: this.thumb.width / 2 }) },
    width = 220, height = 28,
    value: number = 0,
    fieldValue = { value },
    min:   number = 0,
    max:   number = 100,
    step:  number = 1,

    input(v: number) { value = v },

    // the fraction the visuals share; drag bookkeeping (root-space anchor)

    frac: number = { (value - min) / ((max - min) || 1) },
    _x0: number = 0,
    _v0: number = 0,

    // The drag latch: the pointer is captured for the gesture, so the drag must
    // survive leaving the thumb's hit chain — the intrinsic `pressed` (chain-based)
    // deliberately would not. Cleared by the captured release.

    dragging: boolean = false,

    quant(v: number) -> number {
        const q = Math.round((v - this.min) / this.step) * this.step + this.min
        return Math.max(this.min, Math.min(this.max, q))
        },

    onPointerDown(e: PointerEvent) {
        if (disabled) return
        dragging = true
        focusVisible = false                     // a press clears the :focus-visible channel (Control's rule)
        Focus.focus(this)                        // a click claims focus (Control's rule — this override must keep it)
        _v0 = quant(min + (max - min) * ((e.x - 9) / (width - 18)))
        input(_v0)
        _x0 = app.pointerX
        },
    onPointerUp()   { dragging = false },
    onPointerMove() { if (dragging) input(quant(_v0 + (app.pointerX - _x0) / (width - 18) * (max - min))) },
    accessibilityIncrement() { if (!disabled) input(quant(value + step)) },
    accessibilityDecrement() { if (!disabled) input(quant(value - step)) },

    // Home and End go to the ends; Page Up and Page Down move a tenth of the range
    // (never less than a step): the slider's keys on every platform

    onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
        const page = Math.max(step, (max - min) / 10)
        if (disabled || (e.key != "Home" && e.key != "End" && e.key != "PageUp" && e.key != "PageDown")) { super.onKeyDown(e); return }
        input(quant(e.key == "Home" ? min : e.key == "End" ? max : value + (e.key == "PageUp" ? page : -page)))
        },

    opacity = { disabled ? (provided("theme").disabledOpacity) : 1 },

    // Mountain View draws the handle as a vertical bar with a gap in the
    // track around it; every other theme uses the round thumb. One token —
    // provided("theme").sliderHandleStyle == "bar" — selects the rendition; the gap is the
    // track being shortened to either side of the handle rather than drawn
    // through it. Data, not a fork.

    barStyle() { return provided("theme").sliderHandleStyle == "bar" },

    // Redmond's thumb: a 20px round grabber, surface-filled, with an accent
    // dot inside that grows a touch on hover/press. One more value of the
    // same token — data, not a fork.

    dotStyle() { return provided("theme").sliderHandleStyle == "dot" },

    track: View [ height = { provided("theme").sliderTrackHeight }, y = center, cornerRadius = { this.height / 2 }, fill = { provided("theme").trackFill ?? provided("theme").control },
        x     = { classroot.barStyle() ? classroot.thumb.x + classroot.thumb.width + 5 : 0 },
        width = { classroot.barStyle() ? Math.max(0, classroot.width - this.x) : classroot.width } ],
    bar: View [ height = { classroot.track.height }, y = { classroot.track.y }, cornerRadius = { this.height / 2 }, fill = { provided("theme").accent },
        width = { classroot.barStyle() ? Math.max(0, classroot.thumb.x - 5) : 9 + (classroot.width - 18) * frac } ],
    thumb: View [
        width  = { classroot.barStyle() ? 5 : classroot.dotStyle() ? 20 : 18 },
        height = { classroot.barStyle() ? 24 : classroot.dotStyle() ? 20 : 18 },
        y      = { classroot.barStyle() ? 2 : classroot.dotStyle() ? 4 : 5 },
        cornerRadius = { this.width / 2 },
        x = { (classroot.width - 18) * frac + (classroot.barStyle() ? 6 : classroot.dotStyle() ? -1 : 0) },
        fill = { classroot.barStyle() ? provided("theme").accent : (provided("theme").sliderThumbFill ?? provided("theme").surface) },
        stroke = { classroot.barStyle() ? stroke(0, provided("theme").accent) : stroke(1, classroot.hovered || classroot.pressed || classroot.dragging ? provided("theme").accent : provided("theme").line) },
        dot: View [ visible = { classroot.dotStyle() },
            width  = { classroot.hovered || classroot.pressed || classroot.dragging ? 14 : 12 },
            height = { this.width }, cornerRadius = { this.width / 2 },
            x = center, y = center, fill = { provided("theme").accent } ]
        ]
    ]
